// Cited from the ALTRAD8UD-1L2T product download table; all rows dated 2026-04-14. The BIOS rows
// are load-bearing for the 2026-08-11 fleet incident: 'Updated RC to 0ACRB_021' is the MEMORY
// REFERENCE CODE, and srv3 fails DDR training on Samsung M386A4G40DM0-CPB 32GB 4Rx4 LRDIMMs while
// running DRAM FW 220414 (2022) under BL1 built 2023-05-17, emitting per-controller WRLVL errors
// and 'DDR initialization failure. Can't fall back!' on all eight controllers. ONE RELEASE, TWO
// PACKAGINGS, not interchangeable: the ROM zip carries a bare 33554432-byte ALTRAD8UD-1L2T_3.10.ROM
// applied from BIOS setup by Instant Flash, unreachable on a host that cannot POST; the TAR zip
// carries obmc-bios-altrad8ud-1l2t-3.10.all.tar, whose MANIFEST declares
// purpose=...VersionPurpose.Host, version=3.10, MachineName=altrad8ud-1l2t, Type=bios, and which
// the BMC UpdateService consumes out-of-band. Every byte count and digest here was MEASURED on
// 2026-08-11 by acquiring the artifact and hashing it, never transcribed: ROM zip 5359089 bytes,
// TAR zip 5359341, inner push tar 33566720. The TAR locator was not published on the product page —
// it was found by probing the vendor download host and confirmed by HTTP 200 plus successful
// extraction, so it is weaker provenance than a page-published link and should be re-derived if
// the vendor reorganizes. The BMC 03.22.00 row remains digest-Absent with an unobserved locator:
// its image_bytes is the page's stated 27.71MB converted, NOT a Content-Length, so it must not be
// treated as a fetchable artifact until its own locator is observed.

// THE EXECUTED PAIR that establishes the BIOS-tar prerequisite. Same 33566720
// bytes, two hosts of this board model, opposite outcomes — which is what makes
// the minimum evidence rather than an inference from the vendor page (which
// states no such dependency at all).
data asrock_altrad8ud_bios_310_push_refused_at_bmc_207: FirmwarePrerequisiteObservation = FirmwarePrerequisiteObservation {
  board: AsrockRackAltrad8ud1l2t,
  subject: HostBios,
  image_kind: OpenBmcWebUiTar,
  gating_target: BmcController,
  gating_version: firmware_semantic_version(major: 2, minor: 7, patch: 0),
  outcome: PushRefusedByTarget {
    diagnostic: "Invalid ExtendedVersion: primary-all. Please check MANIFEST file!",
  },
  observed_at: 1786579200000,
}

data asrock_altrad8ud_bios_310_push_accepted_at_bmc_322: FirmwarePrerequisiteObservation = FirmwarePrerequisiteObservation {
  board: AsrockRackAltrad8ud1l2t,
  subject: HostBios,
  image_kind: OpenBmcWebUiTar,
  gating_target: BmcController,
  gating_version: firmware_semantic_version(major: 3, minor: 22, patch: 0),
  outcome: PushAcceptedByTarget,
  observed_at: 1786582800000,
}

data asrock_altrad8ud_bios_openbmc_prerequisite_evidence: List<FirmwarePrerequisiteObservation> = [
  asrock_altrad8ud_bios_310_push_refused_at_bmc_207,
  asrock_altrad8ud_bios_310_push_accepted_at_bmc_322,
]

// Irreducible rationale only; the executed pair is carried as FirmwarePrerequisiteObservation rows
// above, and prerequisite_evidence_verdict checks the declared minimum against them. What those
// rows cannot express: the vendor product page states NO such dependency, so the minimum is an
// executed finding, not a documented one; the refusal on srv3 arrived only AFTER a 33 MB upload
// and a host power-off, and Redfish reported TaskState=Exception with no machine-readable cause,
// so the vendor diagnostic in the refusal row was readable only from the BMC journal over
// authenticated SSH; and the Instant Flash ROM packaging carries NoFirmwarePrerequisite because it
// never passes through the BMC, which is why the prerequisite belongs to the PACKAGING rather
// than the release.

// The DOCUMENTED DIMM populations. A population absent from this list is an
// unsupported configuration whose boot failure proves nothing about the
// modules installed in it — note in particular that the four-module
// population is A1+B1+E1+F1 and is NOT one module per channel.
data asrock_altrad8ud_documented_dimm_populations: List<DimmPopulation> = [
  DimmPopulation { board: AsrockRackAltrad8ud1l2t, slots: ["A1"], authority: extdeps_external_authority_anchor },
  DimmPopulation { board: AsrockRackAltrad8ud1l2t, slots: ["A1", "E1"], authority: extdeps_external_authority_anchor },
  DimmPopulation { board: AsrockRackAltrad8ud1l2t, slots: ["A1", "B1", "E1", "F1"], authority: extdeps_external_authority_anchor },
  DimmPopulation {
    board: AsrockRackAltrad8ud1l2t,
    slots: ["A1", "B1", "C1", "D1", "E1", "F1", "G1", "H1"],
    authority: extdeps_external_authority_anchor,
  },
]

// Board integration facts only: HOW the Ampere consoles are routed. The board
// does not own the meanings of Ampere SCP tokens, and its ordinary AMI POST
// code table is a different namespace that does not decode P012/E588/P029.
data asrock_altrad8ud_scp_console_tcp_port: Nat = 2202

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE BOARD'S MECHANICAL FACTS, converged here from the former extdeps.boards.asrock module. Both
// modules described the SAME physical board against two public surfaces of the same vendor — the
// product page and the manual PDF — the section 3 fork: one concept, two authorities, nothing
// structural keeping their slot counts, socket rows or vendor edges in agreement. The mechanical
// facts are rows in this subject module now, and the product page they were read from is the
// authority this module ALREADY declared as asrock_altrad8ud_download_authority, so the
// convergence removes a module without adding a second citation of one page.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

